    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Sunscorch Regent costs {3}{W}{W}. The player has access to Forest, Seaside Citadel, Canopy Vista, Rishkar (taps for G due to counter), Sunpetal Grove, and Flooded Grove untapped, plus Baird (tapped or untapped unclear but has vigilance so likely untapped). They need 3 generic + WW. Flooded Grove can produce {G/U} + tap for GG, GU, or UU, but they chose to tap it for {C}. Since they need {W}{W} and 3 generic, the colorless mana works fine for generic costs IF they have enough white sources. They have Canopy Vista (W or G), Seaside Citadel (G, W, or U), and Sunpetal Grove (G or W) for white. So they can get WW from two of those three lands and use Flooded Grove for colorless as generic. However, tapping Flooded Grove for just {C} wastes its color-filtering potential. If they tapped it for {G/U} to produce {G}{G}, they'd have more flexibility - they could use the two green mana as generic and free up other lands. The difference is small but real: by producing {C} instead of {G}{G} (paying one green/blue hybrid), they get 1 mana instead of 2 mana, meaning they have less mana available overall. Flooded Grove's activated ability costs {G/U} + tap to produce 2 colored mana, which is a net gain of 1 mana over tapping for just {C}. With Rishkar able to tap for {G}, they could pay the {G/U} cost and net an extra mana, potentially leaving more mana open after casting Regent.",
      "action_taken": "Tapped Flooded Grove for {C} (1 colorless mana)",
      "better_line": "Tap Rishkar for {G}, then use that G to activate Flooded Grove's {G/U},{T} ability for {G}{G} or {G}{U}, netting an additional mana and leaving more resources available after casting Sunscorch Regent",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 227,
      "player": "KimiK2 Sarge",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Deploying Spore Frog before combat is suboptimal sequencing. Spore Frog is a sacrifice-based fog effect that doesn't need to attack, so there's no reason to cast it pre-combat. More importantly, Tenuous Truce on an opponent could provide card advantage, and the choice between the two deserves consideration. However, Spore Frog is a strong defensive card in multiplayer that deters attacks, so the card choice itself isn't clearly wrong. The main issue is that casting a creature before combat when you might want to attack with existing creatures (Rishkar, Baird) telegraphs information unnecessarily, though with Baird having vigilance this matters less.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Spore Frog in precombat main phase",
      "better_line": "Consider casting Spore Frog in post-combat main phase to preserve information. Alternatively, Tenuous Truce on the right opponent could provide more card advantage over multiple turns, though it has the drawback of breaking if you attack that player.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 230,
      "player": "KimiK2 Sarge",
      "decision_index": 168,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Tenuous Truce is sacrificed when you attack the enchanted opponent or they attack you. Gem3F Spike has Curse of Verbosity (likely on someone) and is building toward The Ur-Dragon with Fist of Suns, making them a player you may need to attack soon. If Sarge needs to pressure Spike or Spike attacks Sarge, the Truce immediately dies, wasting the card. Additionally, Spike has Monastery Siege which could be on Dragons mode, and their board is currently less threatening than it will be - giving them extra cards accelerates their Dragon strategy. Enchanting a less threatening opponent like Sonnet Libby (who has a slower artifact-based gameplan and is less likely to attack you given Baird's tax) would likely yield more draw steps before the Truce breaks.",
      "action_taken": "Enchanted Gem3F Spike with Tenuous Truce",
      "better_line": "Enchant Sonnet Libby with Tenuous Truce - Libby is less likely to attack into Baird's tax, and you're less likely to need to attack Libby soon, maximizing the number of cards drawn before the Truce is sacrificed",
